### 10.3 Four edges, a known reference and the exact scope of minimality

**Mathematical input 10.3 (The supplied four-edge reference reconstruction).**
Transport Theorem 8.4 already proves the following exact reconstruction.
Its middle probability is this $Z$, its $h_i$ are the current phase
increments, and its edge order is exactly Definition 10.1. Its use here
connects the general graph calculation to the calibrated five-mode source;
it is not an additional reconstruction theorem.
Assume $Z>0$ and use the gauge $\psi_2=\sqrt Z$. From

$$
E^*=\{02,01,03,1\!\text{--}\!13\}
$$

one recovers

$$
\psi_0=\frac{\overline{C_{02}}}{\sqrt Z},\quad
\psi_1=\frac{C_{01}}{\overline{\psi_0}},\quad
\psi_3=\frac{C_{03}}{\overline{\psi_0}},\quad
\psi_{13}=\frac{C_{1,13}}{\overline{\psi_1}}.
\tag{10.5}
$$

The fifth native edge is redundant:

$$
C_{3,13}=C_{1,13}\,\frac{\overline{C_{03}}}{\overline{C_{01}}}.
\tag{10.6}
$$

On an open phase domain away from the walls where the four relative phases
can be independently realized, four instantaneous edges are minimal for a
known positive reference: fewer edges disconnect the graph and leave an
independent component phase. The assertion of four-edge minimality is not
made on a wall where the phase domain itself has lower dimension.

Proof. The first formula fixes the reference-to-$0$ edge and the remaining
three formulas propagate along a spanning tree. Equation (10.6) is direct
substitution. A graph with fewer than four edges on five vertices is
disconnected; a phase rotation of one component leaves all measured edges
unchanged. Off the walls, the supplier's phase-domain result in §8.2
realizes the independent edge phases needed by this lower-bound argument.
The proof concerns pure rays with a calibrated reference, not arbitrary
mixed-state tomography or a complete family of representable functors.
$\square$

The four complex edge values are eight real numbers from which the ten
native real outputs are recovered nonlinearly. Off the walls the current
source has one height and four independent relative phase circles; the
affine capacity of all ten outputs is ten by Transport Theorem 8.6.
These statements are compatible because an affine decoder and a nonlinear
